  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">FemFocus &#x2013; Women, Peace &amp; Security &#x2013; Stichting War Child - MENA - Palestinian Territories (PT), Lebanon and Syria</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The programme will be implemented in the Palestinian Territories (PT), Lebanon and Syria and will contribute to the Women, Peace and Security (sub)objectives of FemFocus. 

The three contexts share a history of conflict, occupation, militarisation, fundamentalism, economic collapse and climate shocks, compounded by patriarchal norms, weak accountability and shrinking civic space. Conflict-related sexual violence (CRSV), early and forced marriage, exploitation and abuse are widespread, while women face intimidation, digital violence, stigma, discriminatory legislation and exclusion from political and peace processes. Their influencing power as leaders, peacebuilders, mediators and humanitarian first responders remains under-recognised, and women-led organisations (WLOs) and women&apos;s rights organisations (WROs) are structurally underfunded. Each context also offers entry points: NAP 1325 policy cycles in PT, Syria&apos;s political transition and Lebanon&apos;s growing women&apos;s municipal representation create momentum for WPS efforts.

War Child will fund and strengthen in-country partners (ICPs): WLOs, WROs and community-based organisations (CBOs). Geographic proximity allows for cross-border exchange on advocacy, mediation, survivor support and digital protection, with War Child acting as a connector. Activities are structured along three pillars:

1. Empowering (young) women leaders to influence peace and security processes through mentoring, ToT workshops and War Child&apos;s VoiceMore methodology, with a focus on conflict prevention, human rights, mediation, and engaging men and boys. Experienced women mediators are connected with youth leaders.
2. Enhancing meaningful participation in decision-making by funding ICPs to engage in WPS dialogue, supporting safe (digital) advocacy spaces, dismantling patriarchal norms, and influencing a Code of Conduct in the Arab League. ToT trainings, shadow councils, feminist schools and national networks bolster NAP 1325 tracking and accountability.
3. Delivering survivor-centred protection to women and girls, including CRSV survivors and refugees, through legal aid, MHPSS using the BeThere approach, ethical documentation of violations, justice-informed accountability, and a helpline in Syria. Capacity-strengthening focuses on leadership, CRSV prevention and digital safety for women human rights defenders (WHRDs).

Desired results:

1) Women&apos;s leadership and influencing power in peace and security processes is strengthened, with over 8,000 women trained in leadership, peacebuilding and participation.
2) Women&apos;s meaningful participation in formal and informal decision-making spaces is increased, supported by 18 WLOs/WROs receiving multi-year financing, and feminist knowledge products and policy briefs on WPS and NAP 1325.
3) More than 10,200 women and girls, including CRSV survivors, have improved access to coordinated, quality, survivor-centred protection services.</narrative>
  </description>
